      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/problem": "Find the slope of the tangent to the curve $y=\\sqrt{-8x-23}-2{x}^{3}$ at the point where $x=-4.$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "To find the slope at a given point, we have to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point.\n\nLet $f(x)=\\sqrt{-8x-23}.$ We use the chain rule in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet $u=-8x-23.$ Then $f=\\sqrt{u}={u}^{1/2}.$ Differentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}=\\frac{1}{2}{u}^{-1/2}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-8.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the two together gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}}\\cdot (-8) \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{u}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{-8x-23}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the sum rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{-8x-23}}-6x^{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $x=-4,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=-4} & =-\\frac{8}{2\\sqrt{-8(-4)-23}}-6(-4)^{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{3}-96 \\\\  & =-\\frac{292}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
